# Cloudflare-only guard for PROXIED hostnames.
#
# WHAT IT FIXES
# A hostname behind Cloudflare is only protected while traffic actually goes
# through Cloudflare. The origin still answers its own IP, so anyone who knows
# the address can set a Host header and skip the WAF, the edge rate limiting,
# the bot management and the DDoS absorption entirely. The 2026-09-07 audit
# found this open on both shared web hosts (finding H1) for the market, the
# price oracle, the explorer and the wrap desk.
#
# HOW TO USE IT
#   1. Put this file at /etc/caddy/snippets/cf_only.caddy
#   2. Add `import /etc/caddy/snippets/*.caddy` at top level, after the global
#      options block and before the first site block
#   3. Add `import cf_only` as the FIRST line inside each PROXIED site block
#
# APPLY IT PER SITE, NEVER GLOBALLY. On these hosts several names are direct on
# purpose and must keep answering the whole internet:
#   pool.pc.am       miners reach stratum on :3333 at this address, and its cert
#                    is Let's Encrypt, so it needs direct reachability for both
#                    mining and ACME renewal. Guarding it breaks mining.
#   electrum1.pc.am  wallet clients connect directly
#   wrapdesk.pc.am   not proxied at time of writing
#   *.aicontrol.pc.am  a co-tenant project's services
#
# TWO TRAPS THIS FILE ALREADY ACCOUNTS FOR
#
# 1. It is a `handle`, not a bare `respond`. A site that groups its routes in
#    handle blocks makes them mutually exclusive, and a bare respond loses to
#    them -- the guard then returns 200 to a direct probe while looking
#    perfectly configured. That happened on the pool host and was caught only
#    because the fix was probed rather than assumed. Because `import cf_only` is
#    the first line of the block, this handle is evaluated first.
#
# 2. Check how the site gets its certificate before guarding it. These sites use
#    a manual Cloudflare Origin CA cert, so no ACME challenge reaches port 443.
#    A site using Let's Encrypt over TLS-ALPN-01 would stop renewing, and would
#    fail silently roughly 90 days later.
#
# VERIFY, DO NOT ASSUME. After reloading, all three must hold:
#   curl -sI https://<host>/                                    -> 200  (via CF)
#   curl -k -sI --resolve <host>:443:<origin> https://<host>/   -> 403  (direct)
#   every deliberately-direct hostname on the box                -> unchanged
#
# 127.0.0.1/8 and ::1 are allowed so on-box health checks keep working.
# Refresh the ranges with:
#   curl -s https://www.cloudflare.com/ips-v4 https://www.cloudflare.com/ips-v6
# Last refreshed: 2026-09-07
(cf_only) {
	@not_cf not remote_ip 127.0.0.1/8 ::1 173.245.48.0/20 103.21.244.0/22 103.22.200.0/22 103.31.4.0/22 141.101.64.0/18 108.162.192.0/18 190.93.240.0/20 188.114.96.0/20 197.234.240.0/22 198.41.128.0/17 162.158.0.0/15 104.16.0.0/13 104.24.0.0/14 172.64.0.0/13 131.0.72.0/22 2400:cb00::/32 2606:4700::/32 2803:f800::/32 2405:b500::/32 2405:8100::/32 2a06:98c0::/29 2c0f:f248::/32
	handle @not_cf {
		respond "direct-to-origin blocked; use the public hostname" 403 {
			close
		}
	}
}
